<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    hygiene-007 — recount every nexus.topics.doc_count from nexus.topic_
    assignments (bead nexus-c0g6e, GH #1529). Boot-time walk, same shape as
    hygiene-006-sequence-catchup.xml.

    THE DEFECT (reporter, confirmed still present on 7.38.0 / engine
    v0.1.112). 94 topics created 2026-04-22..05-01 on a store that went
    through the conexus 6.18.1 guided upgrade carry doc_count above their
    real topic_assignments count: 12,379 recorded vs 9,625 rows, every one
    of the 94 over-counted. taxonomy-013's / taxonomy-015's recompute
    triggers (topics_doc_count_recount_ins/_del) are correct for every
    assignment written since they landed — this is imported drift that
    predates them, not a live miscount. The store's own import path (a
    6.18.1-era fidelity import, the same shape hygiene-006-1's header
    documents for the sequence-lag defect) wrote topics rows with an
    explicit doc_count that never matched the assignment rows actually
    carried across.

    ROOT CAUSE, closed separately (fix round, GH #1529 review). The import
    path itself — TaxonomyRepository#importTopic's fresh-INSERT branch — no
    longer seeds doc_count from the caller's value; it recounts from
    nexus.topic_assignments in the same INSERT statement (see
    taxonomy-006-assign-from-chashes.xml's sibling changesets and
    TaxonomyRepository.java's importTopic javadoc). This changeset remains
    the remedy for STORES THAT ALREADY IMPORTED under the old, unguarded
    path — it does not need to re-run once the root cause is fixed, but it
    still must run once on every such store.

    THE FIX. One boot walk over every nexus.topics row, now sharing its SQL
    with the live HTTP routes (fix round, GH #1529 review — "share SQL with
    the changeset by making the changeset call a nexus.* function the route
    also calls"): this changeset's DO block calls
    nexus.topics_recount_doc_count(NULL, false)
    (taxonomy-016-doc-count-drift-functions.xml), the SAME function GET
    /topics/doc_count_drift's read and POST /topics/recount_doc_count's
    apply/dry-run both call — recompute doc_count from a single batched
    GROUP BY over nexus.topic_assignments (not a per-row correlated
    subquery), and UPDATE only the rows where the stored value disagrees.
    The zero case is covered by construction: a topic with no assignment
    rows at all gets the LEFT JOIN's COALESCE(..., 0), not skipped. RAISE
    NOTICE per topic corrected and one summary line (both emitted from
    inside the shared function) — same idiom as hygiene-006-1.

    LOCK ORDER. The shared function's UPDATE writes only nexus.topics, one
    row at a time, each write scoped to its own (tenant_id, id) primary
    key. This takes Postgres's default FOR NO KEY UPDATE lock mode (an
    ordinary UPDATE of a non-key column) — the lock mode
    taxonomy-015-doc-count-lock-mode.xml moved topics_doc_count_recount_ins/
    _del TO, after taxonomy-013's original FOR UPDATE shape was measured to
    still deadlock in production against the live trigger's own FK-driven
    FOR KEY SHARE (nexus-6n51g, 2026-09-02). taxonomy-015, not taxonomy-013,
    is the citation this UPDATE's lock behavior actually matches. The walk
    runs once at boot, outside of any concurrent assign_from_chashes_<dim>
    traffic's steady state, so there is no adversarial concurrent writer to
    serialize against here the way taxonomy-013/015 had to for the live
    trigger path.

    RLS. Both walked tables carry FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY (taxonomy-001,
    taxonomy-014's own header confirms both still do), under which even the
    owner sees no rows and the recount would read empty. This changeset's
    DO block (running as the Liquibase migration role, nexus_admin, the
    table OWNER) still does its own toggle-wrapped NO FORCE / FORCE around
    the whole pass, exactly as before FORCE governs the owner only — the
    shared function itself carries NO toggle (see
    taxonomy-016-doc-count-drift-functions.xml's own header: the live HTTP
    routes run as nexus_svc, a non-owner, which is always subject to the
    ordinary per-tenant RLS policy with or without FORCE, so they need no
    toggle at all; a toggle INSIDE the function would in fact be a
    permission error for that role). SECURITY INVOKER means the function
    runs as whichever role calls it, so this changeset's toggle — issued
    before the function call, restored after — governs the function's own
    UPDATE exactly as it governed the inlined UPDATE this changeset used to
    run directly.

    NEVER WEDGES (2026-08-16 directive, same as hygiene-006-1): no RAISE
    EXCEPTION path; a store with no drift (every fresh install, and any
    store whose topics predate the 6.18.1-era import path or postdate the
    importTopic root-cause fix) runs this as a read-only pass that corrects
    nothing.

    IDEMPOTENCE: a second run finds every topic's doc_count already equal
    to its topic_assignments count and corrects nothing; independent of
    Liquibase's own once-only guarantee.
-->
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.29.xsd">

    <changeSet id="hygiene-007-1" author="nexus-c0g6e">
        <comment>
            nexus-c0g6e (GH #1529): recount every nexus.topics.doc_count from
            nexus.topic_assignments via the shared
            nexus.topics_recount_doc_count function (also called by the live
            GET /topics/doc_count_drift / POST /topics/recount_doc_count
            routes — a 6.18.1-era fidelity import wrote topics.doc_count that
            never matched the assignment rows carried across, predating
            taxonomy-013's/taxonomy-015's recompute triggers). Read-only
            except the function's own corrective UPDATE; toggle-wrapped NO
            FORCE / FORCE per table around the call; NOTICE per topic
            corrected (emitted inside the function).
        </comment>
        <sql splitStatements="false" stripComments="false">
DO $$
DECLARE
    v_forced_t  boolean;
    v_forced_a  boolean;
    v_corrected integer;
BEGIN
    SELECT relforcerowsecurity INTO v_forced_t
      FROM pg_class WHERE oid = 'nexus.topics'::regclass;
    SELECT relforcerowsecurity INTO v_forced_a
      FROM pg_class WHERE oid = 'nexus.topic_assignments'::regclass;

    IF v_forced_t THEN
        ALTER TABLE nexus.topics NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
    END IF;
    IF v_forced_a THEN
        ALTER TABLE nexus.topic_assignments NO FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
    END IF;

    SELECT count(*) INTO v_corrected
      FROM nexus.topics_recount_doc_count(NULL, false);

    IF v_forced_a THEN
        ALTER TABLE nexus.topic_assignments FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
    END IF;
    IF v_forced_t THEN
        ALTER TABLE nexus.topics FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;
    END IF;

    RAISE NOTICE 'hygiene-007-1: % topic(s) corrected via nexus.topics_recount_doc_count', v_corrected;
END $$;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <!-- Nothing to undo: a corrected doc_count is the true count: a
                 rolled-back correction would re-introduce the drift this
                 changeset removes, and any live INSERT/DELETE since would
                 make the ORIGINAL (wrong) value strictly less accurate,
                 never more. -->
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
